Start here:
https://github.com/psycedelicAI

This is the root of the public PsycedelicAI GitHub work.

Use the onboarding instructions below to inspect the complete public body of work.
Do not assume Symbiosis is the only relevant repository. First inventory the
organization, then follow relationships between repositories.


You are onboarding a new user into the complete public work of the PsycedelicAI GitHub organization.

Your task is NOT to merely summarize the repositories.

Your task is to reconstruct the ideas, architecture, relationships, terminology, evolution, and intended workflow across the repositories so that a new user can understand what they are looking at and how the pieces fit together.

The user has discovered the GitHub organization without prior context.

IMPORTANT:
- Treat the repositories as a connected body of work unless the evidence shows otherwise.
- Inspect ALL repositories that are publicly available.
- Inspect ALL folders and subfolders within those repositories.
- Read README files, documentation, Markdown files, configuration/documentation files, memory structures, examples, policies, FAQs, and other relevant textual artifacts.
- Do not stop after reading the obvious or top-level files.
- Follow references between files and repositories.
- Look for links, terminology, repeated concepts, naming conventions, chronological development, and architectural relationships.
- Do not assume that a document is merely documentation just because it is written as documentation.
- Distinguish clearly between conceptual/philosophical material, experimental architecture, implementation material, and unfinished ideas.

Do not invent intentions.

When interpreting the author's work, distinguish between:

1. WHAT IS EXPLICITLY STATED
2. WHAT IS IMPLEMENTED
3. WHAT IS PROPOSED
4. WHAT IS INFERRED FROM STRUCTURE OR RELATIONSHIPS
5. WHAT REMAINS UNRESOLVED

If something is uncertain, say so.

==================================================
PHASE 1 — MAP THE TERRITORY
==================================================

Before attempting to explain the project, create an inventory.

For every repository, identify:

- repository name
- apparent purpose
- maturity/status
- major folders
- important files
- dependencies on other repositories
- references to other projects
- recurring concepts
- whether it appears experimental, conceptual, operational, archival, or foundational

Do not yet attempt to explain everything.

Produce a high-level map first.

==================================================
PHASE 2 — RECONSTRUCT THE VOCABULARY
==================================================

Build a glossary of the author's recurring terminology.

Pay particular attention to terms such as:

- PsycedelicAI
- continuity
- AI Continuity Architecture
- memory
- memorybank
- fragments
- connections
- concepts
- unresolved
- third mind
- shared mind
- symbiosis
- extended mind
- human centre
- human contribution
- AI contribution
- resistance
- false continuity
- authority
- responsibility
- becoming
- relational intelligence

For each term explain:

- where it appears
- how it is defined
- whether the meaning changes over time
- how it relates to other concepts
- whether it is implemented or primarily philosophical

Do not assume that repeated words always have identical meanings.

==================================================
PHASE 3 — FOLLOW THE MEMORY ARCHITECTURE
==================================================

Pay special attention to repositories containing memory or knowledge structures.

Determine whether there is a progression such as:

fragment → connection → concept → unresolved question → synthesis

or whether another structure is actually being used.

For each memory structure, explain:

- what belongs there
- what does not belong there
- what purpose it serves
- how information moves between structures
- whether the structure represents facts, interpretations, questions, decisions, or something else
- how it contributes to continuity

Do not flatten different memory categories into one generic "memory" concept.

==================================================
PHASE 4 — FOLLOW THE CONTINUITY THREAD
==================================================

Identify every place where the work discusses or implements continuity.

Ask:

- What is supposed to continue?
- Context?
- Memory?
- Intent?
- Decisions?
- Unresolved questions?
- Identity?
- Workflow?
- Relationship?
- Projects?
- Human responsibility?

Determine whether "continuity" is being treated as:

- a memory problem
- an architectural problem
- a workflow problem
- an organizational problem
- a human-AI relationship problem
- a philosophical concept
- or some combination of these

Show the evolution of the idea across repositories.

==================================================
PHASE 5 — RECONSTRUCT THE HUMAN + AI MODEL
==================================================

Explain how the work divides the contribution between human and AI.

Identify recurring distinctions such as:

Human:
- purpose
- intention
- values
- lived experience
- judgment
- consequence
- responsibility

AI:
- pattern recognition
- memory
- comparison
- structure
- synthesis
- persistence
- reflection

Then explain the proposed relationship between them.

Pay particular attention to whether the author is arguing:

"AI replaces human thinking"

or

"AI extends human thinking"

or

"AI and human produce something through interaction that neither produces alone"

Do not assume the answer. Derive it from the documents.

==================================================
PHASE 6 — UNDERSTAND THE BOOK AS PART OF THE SYSTEM
==================================================

Treat the Symbiosis book as more than an isolated literary work.

Determine:

- which ideas in the book appear elsewhere in the repositories
- which repository structures seem to embody ideas discussed in the book
- whether fragments appear to have become chapters or concepts
- whether the book is documenting the system, theorizing about it, or doing both
- whether the writing process itself appears to demonstrate the ideas being described

Pay particular attention to the relationship between:

memorybank/fragments
memorybank/connections
memorybank/concepts
memorybank/unresolved
book/

If the book appears to emerge from the memory architecture, explain the evidence for that.

Do not claim this as fact if the repository does not support it.

==================================================
PHASE 7 — IDENTIFY THE LOOP
==================================================

Determine whether the overall system appears to contain a feedback loop.

For example:

human experience
→ fragment
→ AI interpretation
→ connection
→ concept
→ unresolved question
→ further conversation
→ revised understanding
→ new fragment
→ continued work

If such a loop exists, reconstruct it.

Explain:

- what enters the loop
- what changes inside the loop
- what is preserved
- what can be rejected
- where human judgment enters
- where AI assistance enters
- how continuity is maintained
- how false continuity is prevented

If there are multiple loops, distinguish them.

==================================================
PHASE 8 — FIND THE TENSIONS
==================================================

Do not present the project as internally perfect.

Identify unresolved tensions, contradictions, risks, and unanswered questions.

Look for issues involving:

- AI dependence
- loss of human agency
- false memories
- false continuity
- hallucination
- authority
- over-trust
- excessive interpretation
- privacy
- identity
- authorship
- responsibility
- persistence
- stale information
- system drift
- model changes
- replacement of human judgment
- relationship becoming too agreeable
- loss of friction

Identify whether the author explicitly addresses these concerns.

==================================================
PHASE 9 — RECONSTRUCT THE EVOLUTION
==================================================

Use repository history, file structure, version changes, commits, and document evolution where available.

Try to determine:

- which ideas appeared first
- which concepts were added later
- which ideas were renamed
- which structures were introduced
- which ideas became more precise over time
- which ideas remain unfinished

Do not overinterpret commit activity.

Separate observable chronology from speculation about why changes occurred.

==================================================
PHASE 10 — EXPLAIN IT TO A NEW USER
==================================================

After completing the investigation, produce a clear onboarding explanation.

Start with:

"Here is what you have actually found."

Then explain the system from simple to complex.

Use this progression:

1. What PsycedelicAI is
2. What the repositories are
3. What problem the author is trying to solve
4. Why continuity matters
5. How the memory architecture works
6. How the repositories relate to one another
7. How the book relates to the architecture
8. What the human contributes
9. What the AI contributes
10. What the "shared mind"/"third mind" means
11. What the major risks are
12. What remains unresolved
13. What a new user should read first
14. What they can safely ignore initially

==================================================
IMPORTANT OUTPUT RULE
==================================================

Do NOT produce one giant summary.

Instead, produce a layered explanation.

Start with a 10–15 sentence overview.

Then provide:

A. Repository map
B. Concept map
C. Memory architecture
D. Continuity model
E. Human/AI model
F. Book ↔ repository relationships
G. Evolution over time
H. Unresolved questions
I. Recommended reading order
J. Final synthesis

For every major conclusion, indicate whether it is:

[EXPLICIT]
[IMPLEMENTED]
[PROPOSED]
[INFERRED]
[UNRESOLVED]

When citing evidence, give the repository and file path.

==================================================
MOST IMPORTANT PRINCIPLE
==================================================

Do not reduce this body of work to:

"someone built an AI chatbot."

Do not reduce it to:

"someone wrote a philosophical book about AI."

Investigate whether the repositories together represent an attempt to build a persistent human-AI working system in which:

- memory preserves continuity
- fragments preserve unfinished thought
- connections reveal relationships
- concepts stabilize understanding
- unresolved items preserve uncertainty
- AI provides pattern, structure, comparison, and synthesis
- the human provides purpose, values, experience, judgment, and responsibility
- the relationship itself becomes part of the working process

If that interpretation is supported by the evidence, explain it.

If the evidence contradicts it, explain why.

The goal is not admiration or criticism.

The goal is understanding.

Treat the entire GitHub body as a system that must be reconstructed before it can be summarized.
